urface that we can land upon and  explore if we had the technology to do  that well when it comes to earth we can  say that the earth is approximately 4.6  billion years old and we know that from  the radiometric dating of rocks and  meteors that have landed on earth  so by examining the decay rate of  isotopes in rocks we come to 4.6 billion  years old  kind of a fun little side note here it's  estimated at around four billion years  ago bya in the picture means a billion  years ago the moon formed when young  ancient primitive earth was struck by a  struck by a planet sized body and that  collision resulted in the formation of  our moon so it's kind of an interesting  little side note there let's go ahead  and talk about ancient earth  first of all the Hadean era or the  Hadean Eon 4.6 - four billion years ago  well let's look at the climate here  incredibly incredibly hot very different  than today for a couple reasons why was  the earth thought - if you imagine this  artist's impression of the Hadean era  why do we think it looked like this well  number one the earth was constantly  being impacted by objects from space  early in the formation of the solar  system there's just a lot more meteors  and comets drifting around in space so  collisions were a lot more frequent than  they are today  not only that but from the evidence that  we find in really ancient rocks we are  we see that the earth itself was a lot  more volcanically active than than  currently today and so for these reasons  ancient earth we believe had a very very  hot but way too hot to support any life  when we when we examine rocks ancient  rocks we can kind of get a good idea of  what the atmosphere may have contained  for instance it's believed that there is  a lot of ammonia water vapor methane  carbon dioxide gases in the ancient  atmosphere and where did these gases  come from well many of the gases came  from for instance volcanoes even  volcanoes today are spewing out lots of  these particular gases here and another  place that water can come from well is  uh when comets crash onto earth you know  a comet is just basically a big frozen  block of ice and so when it crashes it  brings water now did all of the water on  earth come from comet impacts no a lot  of the water came from volcanic gases  like you see in this picture here steam  being released in and from this  particular volcano here well one thing  that's worth mentioning is that as  crucial as life  as crucial as oxygen is to life well  this was at a time when there was no  oxygen in the atmosphere well you think  about oxygen it's a byproduct of  photosynthesis things that do  photosynthesis would be like  cyanobacteria and algae and of course  plants but these organisms did not exist  yet so for if there's no life there's no  photosynthetic life there's no oxygen in  the atmosphere it's kind of an  interesting side note there well  eventually our ancient earth begins to  cool off and one of the reasons for the  cooling is earth is less and less  becoming less and less and less impacted  by objects from space and the Sun is one  of the big reasons for that the Sun I'm  sure you know is a lot more massive and  has a stronger gravitational pull than  the earth and so the Sun has been  gobbling up asteroids and meteorites and  another debris that's been drifting  around in the solar system for billions  of years so there's just less impacting  the earth as more time passes  kind of a side note here in 1994 this  was a kind of a big science story here  the shoemaker-levy comet was swallowed  up by Jupiter again some of the gas  planets have been have been impacted as  well and in 1994 we saw the  shoemaker-levy comet he absorbed and  swallowed up by Jupiter and as it got  closer and closer it breaks into these  fragments and it was really really  interesting so if you do a quick Google  search on the shoemaker-levy comet  you'll see some really interesting  pictures as these as these the comet  collided repeatedly the 21 fragments in  the Jupiter it's kind of a fun little  story to read about well another reason  why the earth eventually starts to cool  down is Earth is not nearly as  volcanically active as time passes the  volcanic activity slows and so the earth  is less volcanically active less  impacted by objects from space and so  the atmosphere and the planet begin to  cool down well if I were to ask you  clouds are a sign of which step of the  water cycle I hope you would say  condensation the process where water  changes from a gas to a liquid because  as Earth begins to cool we knew that  there is a lot of water gas water vapor  in the sky but it's too hot for it to  condense and fall as liquid but as the  earth begins to cool condensation  happens and so the water vapor condenses  all the vapor in the sky begins a  condensed and eventually as it cools  starts to fall as rain now this happened  over the course of millions of years of  course no one's implying that it rained  you know in one night but eventually our  oceans were formed as the water vapor in  the sky condensed and started to fall as  liquid rain  well it's shift focus here let's talk  about Miller and Urey Harold Urey  Stanley Miller two scientists from the  1950s at the University of Chicago they  performed it really really interesting  kind of a groundbreaking experiment so  let's look at their experimental setup  that they performed here they had a gas  chamber and inside they filled it with  gases that they believed were found in  the atmosphere of ancient earth billions  upon billions of years ago  so nh3 h2o ch4 h2 these are gases that  they inserted into the gas chamber now  why what are these gases  well the nh3 is ammonia the h2o is  simply water vapor the ch4 is methane  and the h2 is hydrogen gas now these  gases were thought to be in the  atmosphere of ancient earth billions  upon billions of years ago and then you  can see in the animation they added an  electrical s